      "slug": "pit-log-central-texas-brisket",
      "title": "Pit Log Central Texas Brisket",
      "status": "TEST_V1",
      "family": "brisket",
      "animal": "beef",
      "cuts": [
        "packer-brisket"
      ],
      "description": "Salt, coarse black pepper, post oak, patience. A whole packer smoked at 250 F, wrapped in butcher paper through the stall, pulled probe tender, and rested long above 140 F.",
      "concept_ingredients": [
        "whole packer brisket",
        "kosher salt",
        "coarse black pepper",
        "optional: Pit Log House Brisket Rub"
      ],
      "yield": "A 12 lb packer feeds 12 to 16",
      "prep_time": "30 minutes to trim. Add an overnight dry brine if you have the time.",
      "cook_time_guidance": "10 to 16 hours at 250 F, plus a 1 to 2 hour rest. Estimate. Start earlier than you think.",
      "pit_temp_ref": "planning-pit-temperature-bands",
      "finish_ref": "target-brisket",
      "safety_refs": [
        "safety-whole-muscle-red-meat",
        "handling-danger-zone",
        "handling-rest-is-part-of-safety",
        "handling-two-thermometers"
      ],
      "wood_refs": [
        "oak"
      ],
      "equipment_refs": [
        "leave-in-probe",
        "instant-read-thermometer",
        "butcher-paper",
        "slicing-knife",
        "cooler-holding"
      ],
      "shop_ingredient_categories": [
        "kosher-salt",
        "coarse-black-pepper",
        "butcher-paper"
      ],
      "promised_by_graphic": null,
      "app_log_prefill": {
        "meat_type": "Brisket",
        "wood_type": [
          "Oak"
        ],
        "target_temp": 250,
        "wrap_method": "Butcher Paper",
        "rub": "Salt and pepper"
      },
      "related_cut_refs": [
        "beef/brisket"
      ],
      "review_status": "DRAFT",
      "updated_at": "2026-09-02",
      "ingredient_groups": [
        {
          "name": "Meat",
          "items": [
            {
              "qty": "1",
              "item": "whole packer brisket, 12 to 14 lb, untrimmed"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Rub",
          "items": [
            {
              "qty": "1/4 cup",
              "item": "coarse black pepper",
              "note": "16 mesh if you can find it"
            },
            {
              "qty": "1/4 cup",
              "item": "kosher salt"
            },
            {
              "qty": "1 tbsp",
              "item": "granulated garlic",
              "note": "optional. Central Texas purists skip it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Pit",
          "items": [
            {
              "qty": "as needed",
              "item": "post oak splits or chunks"
            },
            {
              "qty": "1 roll",
              "item": "pink butcher paper"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        "Trim. Leave a quarter inch of fat on the cap. Cut out the hard fat between the point and the flat. Round the edges so nothing burns.",
        "Mix the rub. Coat every side. If you have the time, leave it uncovered in the fridge overnight.",
        "Run the pit at 250 F. Post oak, clean thin smoke. Put the brisket on fat side up, point toward the fire.",
        "Leave it alone for 4 hours. Then spritz with water every hour if the edges look dry.",
        "The stall. Somewhere around 150 to 170 F the temperature stops climbing. It can sit there for hours. That is normal. When the bark is set and dark, usually 165 to 175 F, wrap it tight in butcher paper.",
        "Back on at 250 F. Start checking at 195 F. It is done when a probe slides into the flat with almost no resistance. That is usually between 195 and 205 F. The number is a checkpoint. The feel is the answer. USDA FSIS calls beef safe at 145 F with a 3 minute rest. Brisket passed that hours ago. This window is about collagen, not safety.",
        "Rest. Leave it wrapped on the counter for 30 minutes. Then hold it in a dry cooler or a 150 F oven for 1 to 2 hours. Keep it above 140 F the whole time. That is the USDA line for hot holding.",
        "Slice the flat across the grain, pencil thick. Turn the point 90 degrees and slice it across its own grain, thicker. Serve on paper."
      ],
      "notes": [
        "Untested as a Pit Log recipe. This is the standard Central Texas method. It becomes OFFICIAL after a logged cook.",
        "USDA FSIS calls beef safe at 145 F with a 3 minute rest. Brisket is not tender there. The 195 to 205 F window is about collagen, not safety."
      ],
      "kitchen_tested": false,
      "test_log": []
    },
